const MAHAVAKYAS=[
{
  id:'prajnanam',
  skt:'Prajñānam Brahma',
  trans:'Consciousness is Brahman',
  src:'Aitareya Upanishad 3.3 — Rigveda',
  col:'rgba(201,168,76,1)',
  meaning:'Consciousness is not a property of matter. It is the nature of the universe itself. Matter appears within consciousness — not the other way around. This is the most scientific of the four Mahāvākyas: it places the observer at the centre of all existence.',
  scripture:`<blockquote>"Prajñānam Brahma — Consciousness is Brahman. He who knows this, rising upward from this world, becoming immortal in the heavenly world, becomes immortal."<cite>Aitareya Upanishad 3.3</cite></blockquote><p>The Aitareya Upanishad belongs to the Rigveda, the oldest Veda. This Mahāvākya is the Vedic tradition's answer to the hard problem of consciousness: the problem is not hard — it is dissolved. Consciousness is not produced by the brain. It is what the brain, and the universe, appears in. Prajñā is not ordinary knowing. It is the knowing that is always already present — the knowing that knows itself.</p>`,
  science:`<p>In 2022, a group of 124 scientists signed a paper declaring that the "hard problem of consciousness" — why there is subjective experience — cannot be solved by neuroscience alone. The measurement apparatus (the brain) cannot account for itself. This is precisely Shankarāchārya's argument: the knower cannot be the known. Consciousness cannot be an object of consciousness — it is the subject in which all objects appear.</p><p>Physicist Bernard d'Espagnat (Templeton Prize, 2009): "The doctrine that the world is made up of objects whose existence is independent of human consciousness turns out to be in conflict with quantum mechanics." Prajñānam Brahma — 3,000 years before quantum mechanics — made the same claim.</p>`,
  inner:`<p>Sit quietly. Notice that you are aware. Now ask: what is aware of being aware? Follow this question without thinking it — feel your way toward the one who is asking. You will find that the seeker and the sought are the same awareness. That awareness — right now, as you read this — is what Prajñānam Brahma is pointing at. It was never absent. It requires no special state. It is what is present in every state — including the states in which you forgot it.</p>`
},
{
  id:'aham',
  skt:'Aham Brahmāsmi',
  trans:'I am Brahman',
  src:'Brihadaranyaka Upanishad 1.4.10 — Yajurveda',
  col:'rgba(120,160,220,1)',
  meaning:'The individual self — the "I" — is not a fragment of Brahman, not a reflection, not a child. It IS Brahman. The river and the ocean are made of the same water. The wave is not a lesser ocean. This is the most direct of the four: it is a declaration, not a description.',
  scripture:`<blockquote>"In the beginning, this was Brahman alone, and it knew only itself, saying 'I am Brahman.' Therefore it became all. And whoever among the gods awakened to this, they too became that."<cite>Brihadaranyaka Upanishad 1.4.10</cite></blockquote><p>The Brihadaranyaka Upanishad is the largest and one of the oldest Upanishads, part of the Shukla Yajurveda. The sage Yajnavalkya teaches this to his wife Maitreyi in one of the most extraordinary dialogues in all literature. The "I" in Aham Brahmāsmi is not the ego — it is the pure "I Am" that precedes all qualities, all stories, all names.</p>`,
  science:`<p>In quantum field theory, there is no fundamental difference between a "particle" and the "field" it is a local excitation of. The electron is not a thing in the electromagnetic field — it is a local self-excitation of the field. The field and the electron are the same stuff, in different modes. Aham Brahmāsmi at the quantum level: the individual consciousness and universal consciousness are the same field in different modes. There is no metaphysical gap — only a difference of scale and perspective.</p><p>Physicist Erwin Schrödinger, citing the Upanishads directly: "The total number of minds in the universe is one." He was not speaking metaphorically. He meant it literally, as a consequence of his interpretation of quantum mechanics.</p>`,
  inner:`<p>This Mahāvākya is not an intellectual assertion. It is a recognition that arises when the individual "I" has been followed all the way to its source. Begin: "I am aware." Follow the "I." "I am" — where does the "am" come from? "I" — what is the nature of this "I" before any description is added? Strip away the name, the body, the memories, the preferences, the roles. What remains? A presence. A bare awareness. That — that which remains when everything else has been removed — is what Aham Brahmāsmi is pointing at.</p>`
},
{
  id:'tattvam',
  skt:'Tat Tvam Asi',
  trans:'Thou art That',
  src:'Chandogya Upanishad 6.8.7 — Samaveda',
  col:'rgba(160,200,140,1)',
  meaning:'The most celebrated of all Mahāvākyas. Addressed from teacher to student, from the universe to the individual, from the silence to the seeker: That — Brahman, the ground of all being — is what you already are. Not what you will become. Not what you might achieve. Are.',
  scripture:`<blockquote>"Tat Tvam Asi — Thou art That."<cite>Chandogya Upanishad 6.8.7 (repeated nine times)</cite></blockquote><p>The Chandogya Upanishad (Samaveda) presents this teaching nine times — each repetition preceded by a different illustration: the ocean and its rivers, the salt dissolved in water, the seed containing the tree. Uddhalaka Aruni says to his son Shvetaketu: "That subtle essence which you cannot see, cannot taste, cannot smell — that is the Self. And that is what you are, Shvetaketu." The teaching is repeated because the first eight times, the mind hears it as information. The ninth time, it begins to be recognized as truth.</p>`,
  science:`<p>The holographic principle — proposed by physicists Bekenstein, Hawking, Susskind, and 't Hooft — suggests that all information about a volume of space is encoded in its boundary surface. The universe is a hologram: every part contains, in encoded form, the whole. Tat Tvam Asi is the holographic principle applied to consciousness: every individual consciousness contains, in encoded form, the entirety of Brahman. You are not a part looking at the whole. You are the whole, appearing as a part.</p><p>John Wheeler's Participatory Anthropic Principle: "Observers are necessary to bring the universe into being." The universe requires consciousness to collapse from potential into actual. Tat Tvam Asi: the universe requires you. Not metaphorically. Cosmologically.</p>`,
  inner:`<p>"Tat" — That. The ground of being. The silence behind all sound. The awareness behind all thought. Close your eyes and sense what is most fundamentally present. Not the thoughts — the one in which thoughts arise. Not the feelings — the one in which feelings arise. That. "Tvam" — Thou. You. The one reading this. Not the role, not the story, not the body. The awareness that is aware of having a body. "Asi" — are. Not "will be." Not "can be with effort." Are. Right now. Before this sentence ends. That is what you are.</p>`
},
{
  id:'ayam',
  skt:'Ayam Ātmā Brahma',
  trans:'This Self is Brahman',
  src:'Mandukya Upanishad 1.2 — Atharvaveda',
  col:'rgba(200,140,160,1)',
  meaning:'The most precise and complete of the four. Not "my self is Brahman" — THIS Self. The one that is present right now, in this moment, as the witness of this reading. The Māndūkya Upanishad says this in its second verse — having described OM in its first — because OM and the Self that witnesses OM are the same reality.',
  scripture:`<blockquote>"All this is Brahman — Ayam Ātmā Brahma — This Self is Brahman."<cite>Mandukya Upanishad 1.2</cite></blockquote><p>The Māndūkya Upanishad is 12 verses long — the shortest of the major Upanishads — and was declared by Shankarāchārya to be sufficient for liberation on its own. It maps the four states of consciousness: Vishva (waking), Taijasa (dreaming), Prajña (deep sleep), and Turiya (the fourth — the witness of all three states). The Ātman being pointed at in Ayam Ātmā Brahma is not the Ātman of the waking state, nor of the dream state, nor even of deep sleep — it is the Turiya, the witnessing awareness that is present in all three states without being limited to any of them.</p>`,
  science:`<p>Neuroscience has mapped three states of consciousness — waking, dreaming, and deep sleep — and their corresponding neural signatures. But the "witness" — the awareness that is present in all three states and notices the transitions between them — has no neural correlate. It cannot be found in the brain because it is not produced by the brain. It is what notices the brain's states. Ayam Ātmā Brahma: this witnessing awareness is Brahman itself — not generated, not dependent, not local.</p><p>The problem of personal identity in philosophy: what makes you the same person across time? Not the atoms (completely replaced every few years), not the neurons (constantly dying and being replaced), not the memories (distorted and partial). The only continuous thing is the awareness that witnesses the changes. Ayam Ātmā Brahma: that awareness is Brahman.</p>`,
  inner:`<p>Turiya — the Fourth — is not a state you enter. It is what you always already are. The three states (waking, dreaming, deep sleep) arise and subside within it. You experience each state but you are none of them. You are what is aware of all three. Right now, reading this — there is something present that is aware of the words, aware of the mind interpreting them, aware of the body holding the device. That awareness — not your awareness, not a personal awareness — just awareness, without an owner — Ayam Ātmā Brahma. This very Self. Is Brahman.</p>`
}
];

