// CHAPTER IX — THE LIVING HUM · CYMATICS
// ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
TB_CONTENT.cymatics = `
<div class="tb-eyebrow">Chapter IX · sound has a shape</div>
<h2 class="tb-h">The Living Hum — what sound looks like</h2>

<p class="tb-sk">शब्दब्रह्म।<br>
<em class="tb-sk-tr">śabda-brahman</em> ·
<em class="tb-sk-en">the absolute, manifesting first as sound.</em></p>

<p>In the eighteenth century an old German named <strong>Ernst Chladni</strong> sprinkled sand on a metal plate, drew a violin bow against its edge, and watched the sand crawl across the plate to settle along the lines where the metal was not vibrating. The pattern that emerged was geometric, precise, repeatable — and <em>changed with the pitch</em>. A higher note gave more lines, a lower note gave fewer, but every pitch gave the <em>same pattern</em> every time. <strong>Sound has a shape, and the shape is reproducible.</strong> Chladni did not know what he had stumbled into. <em>The Vedic seers had known for two thousand years.</em></p>

<p>What we now call <strong>cymatics</strong> — the visualisation of acoustic standing waves — is the modern instrument that has caught up to what the Upaniṣads were teaching long before there was sand or steel. <em>Each sound has a form. Each form, when held, generates the sound that produced it.</em> The form and the sound are not two things. <em>Nāda</em> and <em>yantra</em> are one.</p>

<p class="tb-pull">A bīja mantra is not a request sent into the silence. A bīja is the audible version of a geometric structure that already exists inside the body. When you sound it correctly, the structure rings. When the structure rings, the chakra it corresponds to comes online.</p>

<h3 class="tb-sub">The plate — tap a bīja, see its form</h3>

<p>Below is a Chladni plate, calibrated to the seven seed sounds of the seven chakras. <em>Tap any of the bīja.</em> The plate will draw the nodal pattern that corresponds to that frequency — the geometric form the sand would take if you were sounding it on a real plate. <strong>This is what each chakra "looks like" as sound.</strong></p>

<div class="tb-cym-stage">
  <canvas id="tb-cym-canvas"></canvas>
  <div id="tb-cym-name" style="font-family:'Cormorant Garamond',serif;font-style:italic;font-size:15px;color:rgba(255,225,180,0.85);text-align:center;margin-top:10px;">tap a bīja to begin</div>
</div>

<div id="tb-cym-pad" class="tb-cym-pad">
  <button class="tb-cym-key" data-idx="0" style="--c:#D43A1E;"><span class="tb-cym-bija">लं</span><span class="tb-cym-lbl">Mūlādhāra</span></button>
  <button class="tb-cym-key" data-idx="1" style="--c:#E8862C;"><span class="tb-cym-bija">वं</span><span class="tb-cym-lbl">Svādhiṣṭhāna</span></button>
  <button class="tb-cym-key" data-idx="2" style="--c:#EFCB3F;"><span class="tb-cym-bija">रं</span><span class="tb-cym-lbl">Maṇipūra</span></button>
  <button class="tb-cym-key active" data-idx="3" style="--c:#3FA66B;"><span class="tb-cym-bija">यं</span><span class="tb-cym-lbl">Anāhata</span></button>
  <button class="tb-cym-key" data-idx="4" style="--c:#3FA0E0;"><span class="tb-cym-bija">हं</span><span class="tb-cym-lbl">Viśuddha</span></button>
  <button class="tb-cym-key" data-idx="5" style="--c:#5A4DE0;"><span class="tb-cym-bija">ॐ</span><span class="tb-cym-lbl">Ājñā</span></button>
  <button class="tb-cym-key" data-idx="6" style="--c:#E8E2FF;"><span class="tb-cym-bija">ॐ</span><span class="tb-cym-lbl">Sahasrāra</span></button>
</div>

<h3 class="tb-sub">Three points worth holding</h3>

<p><strong>One — the petals are not symbolic.</strong> Texts as old as the <em>Śāradā-tilaka</em> describe each chakra as a lotus with a specific number of petals — 4, 6, 10, 12, 16, 2, 1000. For centuries scholars treated this as decorative numerology. But when you sound the corresponding bīja on a Chladni plate, the pattern that appears has <em>exactly that many lobes</em>. The seers were not being poetic. They were transcribing what they saw in inner vision, which was the same nodal pattern Chladni's sand drew externally. <em>The lotus petals of the chakras are the cymatic mode-shapes of the bīja that sustains them.</em></p>

<p><strong>Two — the body is a resonator.</strong> The skull is a curved bone shell. The chest is an air-filled cavity. The throat is a tube. The abdomen is a soft drum. <em>These are all acoustic instruments.</em> When you sound a bīja, the corresponding part of your body resonates — not metaphorically. Place your palm on your chest and chant <em>yaṃ</em>. Place your palm on your forehead and chant <em>oṃ</em>. You will feel the difference instantly. <strong>Each chakra is the location where its bīja resonates loudest.</strong></p>

<p><strong>Three — Hans Jenny was not the first.</strong> A Swiss physician named <em>Hans Jenny</em> spent the 1960s photographing thousands of cymatic patterns and was startled to find that many of them looked like mandalas, śrī-yantras, and the geometric layouts of religious art across cultures. He died not knowing why. The answer is simple. <em>The mandala is not a stylisation of nature. The mandala is what nature looks like when nature is asked to sing one note for long enough.</em> Mandalas across Hindu, Buddhist, Sufi, and indigenous traditions converge because they are renderings of the same acoustic geometry — some traditions reached it through meditation, others through ritual, all converged on the same shape.</p>

<h3 class="tb-sub">Where Nāda meets Bindu</h3>

<p>Cymatics is the missing rung between sound and form, between mantra and yantra, between <em>Nāda</em> (vibration) and <em>Bindu</em> (point of origin). It is the modern confirmation of the oldest Vedic claim — that the universe begins as sound, and that sound has a shape. <strong>The bīja you chant is not a noise that you make. It is a structure that you ring.</strong> The shape on the plate is the same shape that lives in the body — only externalised, only made visible. <em>Inner geometry and outer geometry are one geometry.</em></p>

<p class="tb-source">— Sources · Ernst Chladni, <em>Entdeckungen über die Theorie des Klanges</em> (1787) · Hans Jenny, <em>Cymatics, Vol. I &amp; II</em> (1967, 1972) · <em>Śāradā-tilaka Tantra</em>, ch.25 on chakra-lotus petal-counts · <em>Lakṣmī Tantra</em> on Nāda-Bindu-Kalā · Patañjali, <em>Yoga Sūtra</em> 1.27 (tasya vācakaḥ praṇavaḥ) · Sir John Woodroffe, <em>The Garland of Letters</em>. Mathematical basis · vibration modes of a circular membrane, J<sub>n</sub>(k r) cos(nθ). Original synthesis by Rahul S.</p>
`;

