     context:'Vairagya Prakarana',
     text:'<p>Suka, the son of Vyasa, was born enlightened — he emerged from the womb already free. He did not cry. He did not grasp. His father Vyasa, the compiler of the Vedas themselves, could not satisfy his questions because Suka\'s doubt was not intellectual — it was existential.</p><p>Vyasa sent him to King Janaka, because sometimes a king can teach what a sage cannot. Janaka lived in the world — ruled, loved, fought — and yet was utterly free. <strong>His liberation did not require renunciation of action. It required renunciation of the one who acts.</strong></p><div class="yv-insight"><div class="yv-insight-text">The Self is not something you attain. It is what remains when you stop pretending to be something else.</div><div class="yv-insight-src">Yoga Vasistha · Vairagya Prakarana</div></div><p>Suka understood: freedom is not a destination. It is the recognition that <em>you were never bound</em>. The chains were imaginary. The prison was a painting. And the prisoner was a character in a story that no one was telling.</p>',
