     text:'<p>Prince Rama, only fifteen years old, returns from a pilgrimage across Bharatavarsha. He has seen everything the world has to offer. And he is shattered by what he sees.</p><p>He tells the court: <em>"Wealth is like a serpent\'s hood — beautiful but deadly. Pleasures are like dreams that end with the morning. The body is a machine that wears out. Relationships are two rivers flowing past each other, touching for a moment."</em></p><div class="yv-insight"><div class="yv-insight-text">I do not desire pleasure. I do not desire liberation. I do not desire anything. I only wish to know: what is real?</div><div class="yv-insight-src">Shree Ram · Yoga Vasistha I.33</div></div><p><strong>This is the question that starts everything.</strong> Not "how do I get enlightened?" Not "how do I escape suffering?" But simply — <em>what is real?</em> Vashisht recognizes that this question, asked with total sincerity, already contains its own answer. The one who can ask "what is real?" is already beyond the unreal.</p>',
