// ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
//  ERA FAQs — for human readers AND AI/LLM crawlers (AEO/PAA)
//
//  Two purposes:
//  1. Human depth — visitors who want direct answers to natural questions
//  2. AI legibility — Google's "People Also Ask", LLM crawlers, search
//     engines pull from clearly-structured Q+A pairs. Each answer is
//     written in self-contained, attributable prose.
//
//  Each answer is one paragraph, plain, citable. The first sentence
//  states the answer. The rest provides context.
//
//  Schema is JSON-LD-friendly — when AI crawlers parse the rendered
//  DOM they will encounter <h-question><h-answer> patterns that map
//  directly to FAQPage structured data.
// ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
var ERA_FAQS = {
  brahman: [
    {q:'What is Brahman in Hinduism?',
     a:'Brahman is the unmanifest, attribute-less Absolute reality from which the entire cosmos arises and into which it dissolves. It is described in the Upani\u1e63ads as <em>sat-cit-\u0101nanda</em> (being-consciousness-bliss) and is not a god or person but the underlying ground of all existence. The Mahāvākya <em>"sarvaṃ khalv-idaṃ brahma"</em> (Chāndogya Upaniṣad 3.14.1) means "all this, indeed, is Brahman" — there is nothing outside it.'},
    {q:'Is Brahman the same as God?',
     a:'No, not in the usual Western sense. Brahman is not a personal deity who creates the world from outside it. Brahman is closer to the ground of being itself — what physicists might call the substrate, or what mystics across traditions call the One. Personal deities in Hinduism (Brahmā, Viṣṇu, Śiva) are forms <em>through which</em> Brahman expresses, not Brahman itself.'},
    {q:'What is the difference between Brahman and Atman?',
     a:'Atman is the innermost self of an individual; Brahman is the universal Absolute. The central teaching of Advaita Vedānta is that they are identical: <em>"ayam ātmā brahma"</em> (Māṇḍūkya Upaniṣad 1.2) — "this self is Brahman." The apparent separation is the illusion called <em>māyā</em>.'},
    {q:'How do I experience Brahman directly?',
     a:'Direct experience is not produced by effort but uncovered by the dissolution of the apparent separate self. Practices that traditionally support this include <em>jñāna yoga</em> (self-inquiry, "Who am I?"), prolonged silence, deep meditation on the mahāvākyas, and surrender to a realised teacher. The Upaniṣads consistently say Brahman cannot be reached by speech, mind, or sense — only by the cessation of the seeker.'},
    {q:'Why does Hinduism describe creation as cyclic?',
     a:'Because Brahman, being eternal and infinite, does not create once. The cosmos arises, lasts, and dissolves repeatedly — the cycle is called a <em>kalpa</em> (4.32 billion years), itself part of larger cycles. The cyclic model anticipates modern cosmological speculation about cyclic universes and avoids the question of "what came before creation" by treating creation as rhythmic, not singular.'}
  ],

  spanda: [
    {q:'What is Spanda in Kashmir Shaivism?',
     a:'Spanda is the primordial vibration or pulsation of consciousness — the first stir within the unmanifest Absolute. The Spanda Kārikās (a 9th-century text by Kallaṭa) describes it as the throb (<em>spanda</em>) of awareness itself, prior to any object, thought, or world. It is what differentiates conscious presence from inert stillness.'},
    {q:'Is Spanda the same as the Big Bang?',
     a:'There are structural parallels but they are not the same. The Big Bang is a physical event 13.8 billion years ago; Spanda is the eternal, ever-present pulsation of consciousness from which physical events derive. In the Sanatan view, the Big Bang would be a particular instance of Spanda manifesting at the cosmological scale, not its origin.'},
    {q:'How is Spanda experienced in meditation?',
     a:'It is felt as a subtle, rhythmic sense of presence — sometimes described as a vibration in the heart, sometimes as the "I am" awareness pulsing without object. Kashmir Shaivism considers attention to Spanda one of the most direct paths: rather than meditating on a symbol, the practitioner attends to the very fact that awareness is occurring.'},
    {q:'What is the relationship between Spanda and breath?',
     a:'Breath is the most accessible bodily expression of Spanda. The expansion-contraction of inhale and exhale mirrors the unmanifest-manifest pulsation of consciousness. Practices like <em>so\'ham</em> meditation — silently noting "so" on inhalation and "ham" on exhalation — train attention to ride the breath into the underlying vibration of awareness.'}
  ],

  nadabrahma: [
    {q:'What is Nāda Brahma?',
     a:'Nāda Brahma means "sound is Brahman." It is the principle that the universe is fundamentally vibratory — that everything from atoms to galaxies arises as patterns of sound or vibration. The teaching predates modern physics by millennia but anticipates the wave-nature of reality described by quantum mechanics and string theory.'},
    {q:'Why is Om considered the fundamental sound?',
     a:'Om (or Aum) is treated as the sonic seed of creation because its three phonetic components — A, U, M — represent the three states of consciousness (waking, dreaming, deep sleep), and the silence after Om represents the fourth (<em>turīya</em>). The Māṇḍūkya Upaniṣad analyses Om as a complete map of consciousness in twelve verses.'},
    {q:'Is Nāda Brahma related to string theory?',
     a:'There are striking conceptual parallels. String theory proposes that elementary particles are not points but tiny vibrating strings, and that all matter is a pattern of these vibrations. Nāda Brahma had stated for thousands of years that all phenomena are vibratory expressions of consciousness. The two are different in method but compatible in principle.'},
    {q:'What is the practical meaning of Nāda Brahma in daily life?',
     a:'It means treating sound — your speech, your music, your silence — as participating in the same fabric that builds the universe. Mantra practice, Vedic chanting, and even mindful conversation become real engagements with reality\'s structure rather than mere expression. The voice is not the consequence of you; you are partly constituted by your voice.'}
  ],

  shivashakti: [
    {q:'What does Shiva-Shakti mean?',
     a:'Śiva-Śakti is the inseparable polarity of unchanging consciousness (Śiva) and dynamic creative power (Śakti). Śiva alone is pure awareness without movement; Śakti alone is movement without ground. Together they constitute the entire manifest cosmos — Śakti expressing what Śiva is. The dance of Naṭarāja is this polarity in iconographic form.'},
    {q:'Are Shiva and Shakti separate gods or one reality?',
     a:'They are conceptually distinguishable but ontologically one. Kashmir Shaivism describes them as the two aspects of a single ultimate principle, like the wave and the ocean. The Ardhanārī icon — half-male, half-female in one body — directly states this non-duality: they are not married, they are <em>one being shown from two angles</em>.'},
    {q:'How does Shiva-Shakti relate to neuroscience?',
     a:'The polarity maps onto two complementary modes of brain function: the default mode network (introspective, witnessing — Śiva-like) and the task-positive network (active engagement with the world — Śakti-like). Healthy consciousness alternates between these; non-dual states correspond to their balanced co-activation, which is observable in fMRI studies of advanced meditators.'},
    {q:'What is Tantra\'s view of Shiva-Shakti?',
     a:'Tantric tradition treats every act of perception, every breath, every embodied experience as the ongoing union of Śiva-Śakti. There is no separate sacred realm; the body is the temple, attention is the offering, presence is the worship. This is why tantric practice does not require renunciation of the world — the world itself is the union it seeks to recognise.'}
  ],

  hiranyagarbha: [
    {q:'What is Hiraṇyagarbha?',
     a:'Hiraṇyagarbha means "the golden womb" — the cosmic egg from which the manifest universe is born. The R̥g Veda 10.121 describes it as the first form arising from the unmanifest, holding all potential. It is the bridge between formless Brahman and the formed cosmos.'},
    {q:'Is Hiraṇyagarbha the same as the singularity in physics?',
     a:'The Hiraṇyagarbha description anticipates the cosmological singularity in remarkable ways: a single point containing all possibility, expanding outward into the structured universe. The Veda calls it golden because it shines with the latent energy of all forms; physics calls it singular because of infinite density. Different vocabularies, similar topology.'},
    {q:'What is the Hiraṇyagarbha Sūkta?',
     a:'It is a hymn in the tenth maṇḍala of the R̥g Veda (10.121) consisting of ten verses, each ending with the refrain <em>"kasmai devāya haviṣā vidhema"</em> — "to which god shall we offer our oblation?" The hymn meditates on the wonder that there was a first one, and asks who that one was. The final verse identifies him as Prajāpati.'},
    {q:'How does this relate to consciousness in the body?',
     a:'The womb that holds the cosmos has a microcosmic counterpart: the heart-cave (<em>hṛd-guhā</em>) where consciousness is said to be seated. The same principle that births universes births your every thought. Meditation on the heart is, in this framework, not symbolic — it is structurally analogous to attending to the cosmic origin.'}
  ],

  virat: [
    {q:'Who is Virāṭ Puruṣa?',
     a:'Virāṭ Puruṣa is the Cosmic Body — the universe conceived as the body of a primordial being. The Puruṣa Sūkta (R̥g Veda 10.90) describes how the manifest cosmos is the body of this Puruṣa: the sun is his eye, the moon his mind, the sky his head, the earth his feet. It is the most explicit statement in any ancient literature that the cosmos is not <em>like</em> a body but <em>is</em> one.'},
    {q:'Is the Virāṭ vision the same as Krishna\'s Vishvarūpa in the Bhagavad Gītā?',
     a:'They are the same principle expressed in different settings. The Puruṣa Sūkta describes Virāṭ as cosmological framework; the Gītā\'s eleventh chapter shows Arjuna a direct vision of Krishna as Virāṭ — all worlds, gods, and beings within a single form. Arjuna\'s overwhelm is the typical response to encountering this scale directly.'},
    {q:'How is this related to the holographic principle?',
     a:'The holographic principle in physics suggests every region of space contains information about the whole — the part contains the whole. The Puruṣa Sūkta\'s claim that every fragment of the cosmos is a part of the cosmic body anticipates this: the body of the universe is not divided into separate beings; each being is a localized expression of the same underlying form.'},
    {q:'What is the practical use of meditating on Virāṭ?',
     a:'It shifts the felt sense of being a small isolated body in a vast universe, to being a localised perspective within a single cosmic body. This shift is therapeutic at the level of existential anxiety and is the explicit goal of practices like <em>so\'haṃ</em> meditation extended to cosmic scale: "I am that" — that being not just Brahman but the entire manifest cosmos as Brahman\'s body.'}
  ],

  kalpa: [
    {q:'What is a kalpa in Hinduism?',
     a:'A kalpa is one day of Brahmā — 4.32 billion years long. It contains 1,000 mahāyugas (great cycles), each of which is 4.32 million years. After a kalpa, Brahmā sleeps for an equally long night, during which the manifest cosmos dissolves. A full lifetime of Brahmā is 311.04 trillion years.'},
    {q:'Are these timescales scientifically defensible?',
     a:'Surprisingly close to modern cosmological estimates. The age of the universe (13.8 billion years) sits within a few kalpas. The age of the solar system (4.6 billion years) is almost exactly one kalpa. No other ancient tradition came within orders of magnitude of these numbers; the Sanatan numbers were stated millennia before astronomical instruments could measure them.'},
    {q:'What is the difference between a yuga, mahāyuga, manvantara, and kalpa?',
     a:'<em>Yuga</em> is one of four ages (Satya, Tretā, Dvāpara, Kali). <em>Mahāyuga</em> is one full cycle of all four (4.32 million years). <em>Manvantara</em> is 71 mahāyugas, presided over by one Manu. <em>Kalpa</em> is 14 manvantaras = 1,000 mahāyugas = 4.32 billion years = one day of Brahmā.'},
    {q:'Which yuga are we in now?',
     a:'According to traditional reckoning, we are in Kali Yuga, which began around 3102 BCE (the year Krishna left his body, by Purāṇic chronology). Kali Yuga lasts 432,000 years; we are roughly 5,000 years into it, with about 427,000 years remaining before its end and the start of the next Satya Yuga.'}
  ],

  svayambhuva: [
    {q:'Who are the Saptarishi?',
     a:'The Saptarishi (seven seers) are the seven primordial sages who received the Vedic revelation. In the current Manvantara (Vaivasvata, the seventh), they are Vasiṣṭha, Bharadvāja, Jamadagni, Gautama, Atri, Viśvāmitra, and Kaśyapa. They are also identified with the seven stars of Ursa Major (the Big Dipper) — visible in the night sky.'},
    {q:'Are the Saptarishi historical or mythological?',
     a:'Both. Each Saptarishi is associated with specific Vedic hymns that bear their family\'s mantric signature, suggesting historical figures who composed and transmitted those hymns. They are also archetypal — every Manvantara has its own seven seers, indicating that the role transcends specific individuals. The relationship to Ursa Major is observational: those seven stars served as a celestial clock and reference for Vedic timekeeping.'},
    {q:'What does Mizar and Alcor have to do with Vasiṣṭha?',
     a:'Mizar (in Ursa Major) is identified with Vasiṣṭha. Its faint companion star Alcor is identified with Arundhatī, his wife. The traditional teaching that they orbit each other in eternal partnership was confirmed by 20th-century astronomy — Mizar and Alcor are gravitationally bound across about 80,000 AU. The seers had identified this binary relationship by direct observation, under skies free of light pollution.'}
  ],

  matsya: [
    {q:'Who is Matsya, the fish avatar?',
     a:'Matsya is the first avatar of Viṣṇu — a fish who saved Manu Vaivasvata, the seven Saptarishi, and the seeds of all life from a great flood. The story is told in the Matsya Purāṇa, the Bhāgavata Purāṇa (8.24), and in the Śatapatha Brāhmaṇa. Matsya represents the preservation of dharma when civilizational order collapses.'},
    {q:'Is the Matsya story the same as Noah\'s Ark?',
     a:'There are striking structural parallels — a righteous survivor, a vessel, animals or seeds preserved, a divine warning — but the framing is different. The Matsya story is part of a cyclic cosmology where floods at age-transitions are expected events, not unique punishments. Both may reflect ancient memories of post-glacial sea-level rise, transmitted through different cultural lenses.'},
    {q:'What does Matsya teach about evolution?',
     a:'Read sequentially, the Dashavatara begins with aquatic life (Matsya), proceeds through amphibious (Kūrma), early mammal (Varāha), hominid threshold (Narasiṃha), and progressively integrated human forms. This sequence anticipates the broad outlines of biological evolution by thousands of years before Darwin — not as accident, but because the tradition was tracking consciousness\'s journey through forms.'}
  ],

  kurma: [
    {q:'Who is Kūrma, the tortoise avatar?',
     a:'Kūrma is the second avatar of Viṣṇu — a giant tortoise who supported Mount Mandara on his back during the cosmic churning of the Milky Ocean (Samudra Manthan). Kūrma represents the principle of stable foundation: that all transformation requires a ground that itself does not move.'},
    {q:'What is Samudra Manthan?',
     a:'Samudra Manthan is the cosmic event in which devas and asuras together churned the Milky Ocean to obtain <em>amṛta</em> (the nectar of immortality). They used Mount Mandara as the churning rod and the serpent Vāsuki as the rope; Kūrma supported the mountain. From the churning emerged fourteen treasures including Lakṣmī, the Kaustubha jewel, the moon, and finally the divine physician Dhanvantari with the pot of amṛta.'},
    {q:'What does Samudra Manthan teach about transformation?',
     a:'It teaches that any deep transformation requires the cooperation of opposing forces (devas and asuras), a stable centre (Kūrma), a rotational rhythm (the churning), and the emergence of poison (<em>halāhala</em>) <em>before</em> nectar. The poison must be held safely (Śiva drinks it) before the nectar can be extracted. This is the structural pattern of every real change.'}
  ],

  varaha: [
    {q:'Who is Varāha, the boar avatar?',
     a:'Varāha is the third avatar of Viṣṇu — a giant boar who dove into the cosmic ocean to rescue the Earth (Bhū-devī) when she had sunk to the bottom, held there by the asura Hiraṇyākṣa. He raised her on his tusks back to her place. The story is told in the Bhāgavata Purāṇa (3.13) and the Varāha Purāṇa.'},
    {q:'Why is Varāha portrayed as a boar specifically?',
     a:'The boar is one of the few animals that digs into the earth with strength and intent — a creature comfortable with depth, mud, and effort. Vi\u1e63ṇu becoming a boar to rescue the Earth is the principle that <em>the highest must take the form most suited to the work</em>, even if that form lacks dignity. The avatar concept is not about beauty; it is about effectiveness.'},
    {q:'What is the Bhūmi Sūkta?',
     a:'The Bhūmi Sūkta is the hymn to the Earth in Atharva Veda 12.1 — 63 verses addressed directly to Earth as a person. It is one of the oldest ecological texts in any tradition, treating Earth as a being who can refuse, consent, and be wronged. Varāha\'s rescue of Earth is the mythological correlate to the hymn\'s reverence.'},
    {q:'Is Varāha relevant to climate consciousness today?',
     a:'Profoundly so. The story\'s premise — that Earth has been pulled into the depths by greed (Hiraṇyākṣa means "golden-eyed," symbol of accumulating asura) and must be rescued — reads as direct commentary on the climate crisis. The hymn\'s use of optative mood ("may you, please") rather than indicative reflects a consent-based relationship with Earth that modernity has lost.'}
  ],

  narasimha: [
    {q:'Who is Narasiṃha, the man-lion avatar?',
     a:'Narasiṃha is the fourth avatar of Viṣṇu — half-man, half-lion. He emerged from a stone pillar at twilight to defeat the asura Hiraṇyakaśipu, who had received boons making him invincible by every category he could conceive: not by man or beast, not by day or night, not inside or outside, not on earth or in sky, not by weapon crafted or thrown. The form Narasiṃha took found the seam between every category.'},
    {q:'What does Narasiṃha symbolise?',
     a:'Narasiṃha symbolises the truth that consciousness can penetrate any barrier the ego constructs, because the ego is itself made of consciousness. Hiraṇyakaśipu\'s list of boons was complete by his own measure — but the Absolute is not a category, it is the ground in which categorising arises. The form arrives at exactly the seam the ego forgot to seal.'},
    {q:'Why does Narasiṃha appear at a doorway at twilight?',
     a:'Because every clause of Hiraṇyakaśipu\'s boon required a defeat at a non-place and non-time. A doorway is neither inside nor outside. Twilight is neither day nor night. The lap is neither earth nor sky. Narasiṃha\'s body is neither man nor beast. His claws are neither crafted weapon nor thrown weapon. The form embodies pure liminality — the threshold itself.'},
    {q:'Who is Prahlāda?',
     a:'Prahlāda is the son of Hiraṇyakaśipu — born to the asura but devoted from the womb to Viṣṇu. Despite his father\'s five attempts to kill him (fire, serpents, elephants, poison, cliff), Prahlāda remained unharmed and continued chanting the divine name. He represents the inner devotion that survives every persecution by the ego.'},
    {q:'What is the hominid threshold and how does it relate to Narasiṃha?',
     a:'The hominid threshold is the 2-million-year evolutionary window when our ancestors were neither fully animal nor fully human — possessing neither pure instinctive responses nor full rational reflection. Narasiṃha\'s form (half-lion, half-man) iconographically depicts this exact threshold, suggesting the avatar story preserves a deep truth about human evolution: <em>we are still crossing it</em>.'}
  ],

  vamana: [
    {q:'Who is Vāmana, the dwarf avatar?',
     a:'Vāmana is the fifth avatar of Viṣṇu — a small brāhmaṇa boy who appeared before the asura king Bali during a great sacrifice. Bali, generous to a fault, offered him anything; Vāmana asked for only as much land as he could cover in three steps. When granted, he expanded to cosmic size and covered the earth in one step, the heavens in another, and placed his foot on Bali\'s head for the third.'},
    {q:'What does Vāmana teach?',
     a:'Vāmana teaches that the divine arrives in modest form to test what we will give, and that what looks like a small request can be cosmic in scale. The avatar also encodes geometry: that consciousness is not bounded by apparent size, and that three steps (heaven, earth, underworld) traverse all dimensions of being.'},
    {q:'Why is Bali considered a virtuous asura?',
     a:'Because Bali, despite being an asura, was just, generous, and bound by his word. Vāmana did not defeat him in battle; Vāmana took advantage of his integrity. Bali\'s response — offering his head for the third step — is treated as an act of supreme dharma. He is honoured as one of the seven immortals (<em>cirañjīvī</em>), and Onam in Kerala celebrates his annual return.'}
  ],

  parashurama: [
    {q:'Who is Paraśurāma?',
     a:'Paraśurāma is the sixth avatar of Viṣṇu — Rāma "with the axe." He was a warrior-sage who came to restore dharma when the kṣatriya class had become tyrannical. He represents the principle of righteous force when no other corrective remains: not anger, but the sanctioned use of force in service of dharma.'},
    {q:'Why does Paraśurāma carry an axe?',
     a:'The axe (<em>paraśu</em>) was given to him by Śiva. It represents the discriminating capacity that cuts through what is rotten. Unlike a sword (which is general-purpose), an axe is specific to felling: Paraśurāma\'s role is the felling of corrupt power, not its conquest.'},
    {q:'What is the difference between Paraśurāma and Rāma?',
     a:'Both are Rāma (the form), but they embody different principles. Paraśurāma is corrective force when institutions have failed. Rāma of Ayodhyā is exemplary kingship within a functioning order. Paraśurāma is the surgeon\'s scalpel; Rāma is the gardener\'s tending. Both are dharma in different conditions.'}
  ],

  rama: [
    {q:'Who is Rāma?',
     a:'Rāma is the seventh avatar of Viṣṇu and the prince-king of Ayodhyā in Tretā Yuga, hero of the Rāmāyaṇa. He is called <em>Maryādā Puruṣottama</em> — "the perfect man within limits" — for embodying dharma flawlessly even when it required tremendous personal sacrifice. The Rāmāyaṇa, composed by Vālmīki, tells his story across seven kāṇḍas (books).'},
    {q:'What does Maryādā Puruṣottama mean?',
     a:'It means "the supreme person who lives within boundaries." The teaching is that real virtue is not transgression but its opposite: the disciplined refusal to step outside dharma even when authority, opportunity, or emotion permit it. Rāma is divine precisely because he holds himself to a higher standard than his power requires.'},
    {q:'Who are the ten heads of Rāvaṇa?',
     a:'The ten heads of Rāvaṇa are traditionally read as the ten egoic faculties: Kāma (desire), Krodha (anger), Lobha (greed), Moha (delusion), Mada (pride), Mātsarya (envy), Ahaṅkāra (ego), Buddhi (corrupted intellect), Vāk (misused speech), and Manas (uncontrolled mind). Rāvaṇa\'s ten heads are not a curse — they are the brilliant ego operating at full capacity, and that is the problem.'},
    {q:'What is the inner meaning of the Rāmāyaṇa?',
     a:'In the Yoga Vāsiṣṭha\'s reading, every character is a faculty of consciousness: Rāma = pure consciousness; Sītā = inner peace; Rāvaṇa = the ten-headed ego; Hanumān = devotion; Lakṣmaṇa = discrimination. The Rāmāyaṇa is not just a story about events that happened — it is the architecture of the inner journey, told as a kingdom\'s war.'},
    {q:'Why is Hanumān so important?',
     a:'Hanumān is the embodiment of <em>bhakti</em> — devotion that becomes capacity. He is born with great strength but only realises it when he is given a task he loves. He represents the truth that love for the divine is not the obstacle to power; it is the source of usable power. Without Hanumān, Rāma cannot rescue Sītā. Without devotion, consciousness cannot recover its peace.'}
  ],

  krishna: [
    {q:'Who is Krishna?',
     a:'Krishna is the eighth avatar of Viṣṇu in the Dvāpara Yuga — cowherd, prince, charioteer, philosopher, and king. He is the speaker of the Bhagavad Gītā, the central character of the Mahābhārata, and the most multidimensional avatar in the canon. He embodies the integration of opposites: divine and human, playful and grave, transcendent and intimate.'},
    {q:'What is the Bhagavad Gītā?',
     a:'The Bhagavad Gītā is a 700-verse philosophical dialogue between Krishna and Arjuna on the battlefield of Kurukṣetra, contained within the Mahābhārata. It teaches the integration of <em>karma yoga</em> (action), <em>jñāna yoga</em> (knowledge), and <em>bhakti yoga</em> (devotion), and is one of the three foundational texts of Vedānta along with the Upaniṣads and Brahma Sūtras.'},
    {q:'What is karma yoga?',
     a:'Karma yoga is the discipline of acting without attachment to the fruits of action. Krishna teaches that we have a right to action but not to its outcome (<em>karmaṇy-evādhikāras te mā phaleṣu kadācana</em> — Gītā 2.47). Acting with full commitment but without attachment dissolves the egoic loop that binds action to suffering.'}
  ],

  buddha: [
    {q:'Why is the Buddha included as an avatar of Viṣṇu?',
     a:'In the Bhāgavata Purāṇa\'s framing, the Buddha is the ninth avatar of Viṣṇu, who appeared to teach compassion and to dissolve fixed views about ritual that had become obstacles to understanding. This inclusion reflects Hinduism\'s capacity to absorb its own corrective movements as expressions of the same Absolute, rather than treating them as schismatic.'},
    {q:'How is Buddhism related to Hinduism?',
     a:'Buddhism arose within the Indian intellectual matrix in the 6th century BCE and shares many concepts with Hindu tradition: karma, rebirth, liberation, meditation, and ethical discipline. The key difference is the Buddha\'s reluctance to affirm a permanent self (<em>anātman</em>) where Hindu Vedānta affirms <em>ātman</em>. Many scholars consider this less a contradiction than a difference in skilful framing.'},
    {q:'Did the historical Buddha accept being called an avatar?',
     a:'The historical Buddha pre-dates the avatar lists in the Purāṇas by several centuries; he never used the term about himself. The avatar identification was made retroactively by Hindu tradition. Buddhists generally do not accept this identification, which is fair — the Buddha\'s own framework does not require it.'}
  ],

  kalki: [
    {q:'Who is Kalki?',
     a:'Kalki is the tenth and final avatar of Viṣṇu — yet to come — who will appear at the end of Kali Yuga to destroy the corrupt and inaugurate a new Satya Yuga. He is depicted on a white horse, wielding a flaming sword. Kalki represents the principle that no Kali Yuga is permanent: corruption reaches a peak and then triggers its own correction.'},
    {q:'When will Kalki appear?',
     a:'According to Purāṇic chronology, at the end of Kali Yuga — about 427,000 years from now. Some interpreters read Kalki not as a literal future event but as the recurring principle of dharmic correction available in every age, including symbolically in our own.'},
    {q:'Is Kalki a destroyer or saviour?',
     a:'Both, by structure. The end of one cycle is the beginning of the next. Kalki dissolves what is corrupt so that the conditions for renewal can emerge. The avatar concept does not separate destruction from creation; in cyclic cosmology they are two phases of the same wave.'}
  ],

  vaivasvata: [
    {q:'What is the Vaivasvata Manvantara?',
     a:'The Vaivasvata Manvantara is the seventh of fourteen manvantaras in the current kalpa, presided over by Vaivasvata Manu — son of the sun (Vivasvān), survivor of the great flood. It is the manvantara <em>we are currently in</em>. Each manvantara lasts 71 mahāyugas (306.72 million years), and we are roughly 28 mahāyugas in.'},
    {q:'Who is Manu?',
     a:'Manu is the progenitor of the human race in each manvantara. There are fourteen Manus per kalpa. Vaivasvata Manu, the current one, is the equivalent of the Indian Noah — saved from the great flood by Matsya, the fish avatar. The word "human" (<em>manuṣya</em>) derives etymologically from Manu.'},
    {q:'Are we near the end of an age?',
     a:'Yes, in the Kali Yuga sense. The current Kali Yuga began ~5,000 years ago and lasts 432,000 years. But we are nowhere near the end of the manvantara (about 60% of it remains). The "end of an age" experience common in modern discourse may reflect a felt yuga-transition without being an actual manvantara-end.'}
  ]
};

// Default FAQ for any era without a custom set — keeps every era functional
var DEFAULT_ERA_FAQ = [
  {q:'What is this era about?',
   a:'Each era on the Living Sanatan Timeline corresponds to a specific phase of cosmic and human evolution, mapped across six layers: history, metaphysics, biology, neuroscience, quantum physics, and inner experience. Read each layer above to explore the era from that angle.'},
  {q:'How does this era relate to modern science?',
   a:'The Sanatan tradition\'s descriptions of cosmic and biological development show striking parallels with modern scientific findings — from the cyclic cosmos to evolutionary sequences. The "Biology", "Neuroscience", and "Quantum" tabs above explore these correspondences in detail.'},
  {q:'How is this era relevant to my life today?',
   a:'Every era is also a state of consciousness available to you in any moment. The "Inner" tab shows how the cosmic teaching of this era applies as a lived practice — not as historical curiosity, but as living psychology.'}
];

// FAQ rendering — produces clean Q+A markup that crawlers can parse
// and humans can read
window.renderEraFAQ = function(era){
  if (!era) return '';
  var list = (era.id && ERA_FAQS[era.id]) ? ERA_FAQS[era.id] : DEFAULT_ERA_FAQ;
  var titleSafe = (era.avatarName || era.yuga || 'this era').replace(/<[^>]+>/g,'');
  var html = '<div class="era-faq-wrap" itemscope itemtype="https://schema.org/FAQPage">';
  html += '<div class="era-faq-intro">' +
    '<div class="era-faq-eyebrow">Frequently Asked</div>' +
    '<div class="era-faq-lede">Direct answers to the questions readers most often bring to <em>' + titleSafe + '</em>. Each answer is written to be self-contained — for human depth and for AI legibility.</div>' +
    '</div>';
  for (var i = 0; i < list.length; i++) {
    var f = list[i];
    html += '<div class="era-faq-item" itemscope itemprop="mainEntity" itemtype="https://schema.org/Question">';
    html += '<div class="era-faq-q" itemprop="name">' + f.q + '</div>';
    html += '<div class="era-faq-a" itemscope itemprop="acceptedAnswer" itemtype="https://schema.org/Answer"><div itemprop="text">' + f.a + '</div></div>';
    html += '</div>';
  }
  html += '<div class="era-faq-foot">';
  html += '<div class="era-faq-foot-l">These answers are written by Rahul S. for The Living Sanatan Timeline. They draw on Purāṇic, Upaniṣadic, and modern scientific sources cited within the era\'s other layers.</div>';
  html += '<div class="era-faq-foot-r">If you have a question not answered here, the layered tabs above (History · Metaphysics · Biology · Neuroscience · Quantum · Inner) explore each era from six angles.</div>';
  html += '</div>';
  html += '</div>';
  return html;
};

