// THE TAB CONTENT — PHILOSOPHY · TYPES · 100 THINGS · CELL · NOTES
// ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
var DHARM_TAB_CONTENT={
'what': {
  title:'What Dharm Is — In My Words',
  body: dhBuildWhatDharmIs()
},
'five': {
  title:'The Five Dharmas',
  body: dhBuildFiveDharmas()
},
'panth': {
  title:'Dharm vs Religion (Panth)',
  body: dhBuildPanth()
},
'thousand': {
  title:'The Hundred Things and Their Dharm',
  body: dhBuildThousand()
},
'cell': {
  title:'The Cell That Heals',
  body: dhBuildCell()
},
'notes': {
  title:'Source · Notes · References',
  body: dhBuildNotes()
}
};

// ── What Dharm Is ──
function dhBuildWhatDharmIs(){
  return '\
<p style="font-family:\'Cormorant Garamond\',serif;font-size:18px;line-height:1.85;color:rgba(245,230,210,.95);font-style:italic;margin-bottom:30px;">\
Forget — for a moment — every priest, every temple, every ritual, every translation that ever told you what dharm is. Sit with the word as if you had never heard it before. Then watch what is in front of you.\
</p>\
<p style="font-family:\'Cormorant Garamond\',serif;font-size:16.5px;line-height:1.85;color:rgba(225,215,190,.9);">\
A flame is lit. <strong>It burns.</strong> Without being told. Without negotiation. It does not need to be reminded that it is fire. <em>It is simply doing what fire does.</em></p>\
<p style="font-family:\'Cormorant Garamond\',serif;font-size:16.5px;line-height:1.85;color:rgba(225,215,190,.9);">\
A drop of water lands on a leaf. <strong>It wets the leaf.</strong> No instruction. No doctrine. No congregation. The water does not consult a scripture before becoming wet. <em>It is simply doing what water does.</em></p>\
<p style="font-family:\'Cormorant Garamond\',serif;font-size:16.5px;line-height:1.85;color:rgba(225,215,190,.9);">\
Your hand is cut. The first cell at the edge of the wound — without anyone\'s instruction — already begins repair. <strong>That cell does not know it is part of a body. It does not know it is alive. It does not know what life means. It simply does what a cell at the edge of a wound does.</strong> And in doing it, the body lives.\
</p>\
<h3 style="font-family:\'Cinzel\',serif;font-size:14px;letter-spacing:3px;color:rgba(255,210,130,.95);margin:32px 0 12px;">THIS IS DHARM.</h3>\
<p style="font-family:\'Cormorant Garamond\',serif;font-size:16.5px;line-height:1.85;color:rgba(225,215,190,.9);">\
Dharm is not <em>religion</em>. Dharm is not <em>a doctrine</em>. Dharm is not <em>a set of commandments handed down from a sky</em>. Dharm is not <em>a checklist of acceptable behaviours</em>. Dharm is not <em>a club you join</em>. Dharm is not <em>a flag you fly</em>. Dharm is not <em>a side in a war.</em>\
</p>\
<p style="font-family:\'Cormorant Garamond\',serif;font-size:18px;line-height:1.85;color:rgba(255,225,180,.95);text-align:center;font-style:italic;margin:36px 0;border-top:1px solid rgba(220,160,80,.18);border-bottom:1px solid rgba(220,160,80,.18);padding:24px 12px;">\
Dharm is the <strong>foundational, eternal, just, pragmatic nature</strong> of a thing — by which it does what it does, without instruction, and by which reality itself is made.\
</p>\
<p style="font-family:\'Cormorant Garamond\',serif;font-size:16.5px;line-height:1.85;color:rgba(225,215,190,.9);">\
Fire <em>can only burn</em>. That is its dharm. <strong>If fire stopped burning, it would not be fire.</strong> Water <em>can only wet</em>. That is its dharm. If water stopped wetting, it would not be water. Life <em>blooms</em> — and the bloom contains the seed of the end, and the end contains the seed of the next bloom. <strong>This is the dharm of life. Not a rule about life. The very nature by which life is life.</strong>\
</p>\
<p style="font-family:\'Cormorant Garamond\',serif;font-size:16.5px;line-height:1.85;color:rgba(225,215,190,.9);">\
The first cell at the edge of the wound is not following the Bhagavad Gītā. It has never read the Vedas. It has no theology. It has no priest. <strong>And yet it is doing dharm — perfectly, instantly, without complaint, without expectation of reward.</strong> <em>This is the cell\'s dharm. To repair. To divide when whole. To die when commanded.</em>\
</p>\
<h3 style="font-family:\'Cinzel\',serif;font-size:14px;letter-spacing:3px;color:rgba(255,210,130,.95);margin:32px 0 12px;">DHARM IS WHAT MAKES THE THING WHAT IT IS.</h3>\
<p style="font-family:\'Cormorant Garamond\',serif;font-size:16.5px;line-height:1.85;color:rgba(225,215,190,.9);">\
The dharm of a tree is to grow upward against gravity, to give shade to whoever sits beneath, to offer fruit to whoever passes, to die one day standing if it can. The dharm of a mother is to nourish without expectation, to protect, and — exactly when ready — to let the child go. The dharm of a teacher is to give, even if not received. The dharm of a warrior is to protect, even at the cost of life. The dharm of time is to move forward and never backward.\
</p>\
<p style="font-family:\'Cormorant Garamond\',serif;font-size:16.5px;line-height:1.85;color:rgba(225,215,190,.9);">\
<strong>None of these are imposed from outside.</strong> They are the very <em>nature</em> of the thing. They are the way the thing fulfils what it is.\
</p>\
<p style="font-family:\'Cormorant Garamond\',serif;font-size:16.5px;line-height:1.85;color:rgba(225,215,190,.9);">\
This is why dharm is called <em>sanātana</em> — eternal. Not eternal because it was decreed eternal. Eternal because <strong>it is what makes anything be the thing it is</strong>. The fire of ten thousand years ago burnt the same way fire burns today. The water that wet the cup of a Vedic seer wets the cup at your kitchen sink. <em>Dharm doesn\'t change because reality doesn\'t change.</em>\
</p>\
<h3 style="font-family:\'Cinzel\',serif;font-size:14px;letter-spacing:3px;color:rgba(255,210,130,.95);margin:32px 0 12px;">PRAGMATISM AND JUSTICE</h3>\
<p style="font-family:\'Cormorant Garamond\',serif;font-size:16.5px;line-height:1.85;color:rgba(225,215,190,.9);">\
Dharm is <strong>pragmatic</strong>. It is not idealistic. The cell does not heal a wound by chanting affirmations. It heals because that is what works. Fire does not warm the home through wishful thinking. It warms by burning fuel.\
<strong>Dharm is whatever, by being itself, makes the thing function.</strong> If something stops working — stops being what it is — it has lost its dharm.</p>\
<p style="font-family:\'Cormorant Garamond\',serif;font-size:16.5px;line-height:1.85;color:rgba(225,215,190,.9);">\
And dharm is <strong>just</strong>. Not "just" as in punitive, or "just" as in fair-by-human-rules. <em>Just</em> as in: the consequences of being or breaking your own nature are exact. They arrive. There is no escape from them. Fire does not get to be fire and also not burn. A liar does not get to be respected and also lie habitually. A society that abandons its young people\'s capacity to feed themselves does not get to be a society for long. <strong>Action and consequence are the same word, said at two different times.</strong> This is why dharm and karma are the same body, seen from two angles.\
</p>\
<h3 style="font-family:\'Cinzel\',serif;font-size:14px;letter-spacing:3px;color:rgba(255,210,130,.95);margin:32px 0 12px;">TIME AND US</h3>\
<p style="font-family:\'Cormorant Garamond\',serif;font-size:16.5px;line-height:1.85;color:rgba(225,215,190,.9);">\
Dharm includes <strong>time</strong> — because time is itself one of the things that has a dharm. <em>To move forward. To make change irreversible. To carry every cause toward its effect.</em> When you act in line with your dharm, time moves with you; when you act against your nature, time wears you down. This is not a metaphor. <strong>Try to be what you are not, for one decade. The body teaches.</strong>\
</p>\
<p style="font-family:\'Cormorant Garamond\',serif;font-size:16.5px;line-height:1.85;color:rgba(225,215,190,.9);">\
And dharm includes <strong>us</strong> — because we are also things, in the world, with natures. To be human is to be a body, a breath, a memory, a relationship, a role, and a flame of attention that can choose. <em>Our dharm is to discover, by careful living, what we are most truly — and then to do that, fully, until the breath returns to the air it was borrowed from.</em>\
</p>\
<h3 style="font-family:\'Cinzel\',serif;font-size:14px;letter-spacing:3px;color:rgba(255,210,130,.95);margin:32px 0 12px;">THIS IS WHAT CREATES REALITY</h3>\
<p style="font-family:\'Cormorant Garamond\',serif;font-size:16.5px;line-height:1.85;color:rgba(225,215,190,.9);">\
Reality is not a stage on which dharm performs. <strong>Reality is what dharm creates.</strong> When fire burns and water wets and the cell heals and the tree grows and the warrior protects and the teacher gives — all at once, all in their own way, all without instruction — <em>that very pattern, that very functioning, IS the reality you and I find ourselves in</em>. There is no reality "behind" or "above" dharm. The dharm of all things, doing themselves, IS the world.\
</p>\
<p style="font-family:\'Cormorant Garamond\',serif;font-size:16.5px;line-height:1.85;color:rgba(225,215,190,.9);">\
This is why the tradition I was born into never separated <em>physics</em> from <em>ethics</em>, <em>cosmology</em> from <em>conduct</em>, <em>nature</em> from <em>law</em>. <strong>The same word — dharm — covers all of them.</strong> Because the same thing is happening at every level. A river finding its bed is doing dharm. A judge finding the right verdict is doing dharm. A galaxy spiralling around its centre is doing dharm. <em>The pattern is one. Only the costume changes.</em>\
</p>\
<p style="font-family:\'Cormorant Garamond\',serif;font-size:18px;line-height:1.95;color:rgba(255,225,180,.96);text-align:center;font-style:italic;margin:36px 0;padding:0 12px;">\
Dharm is the foundational ground. Religion is one of the costumes. Don\'t confuse the costume with the dancer.\
</p>\
<p style="font-family:\'IBM Plex Mono\',monospace;font-size:8px;letter-spacing:2px;color:rgba(180,140,80,.6);text-transform:uppercase;margin-top:30px;padding-top:18px;border-top:1px solid rgba(220,160,80,.1);">\
References · Manusmṛti II.6 · Mahābhārata 12.109 · Brahmasūtra Bhāṣya · Yājñavalkya Smṛti · Bhagavad Gītā 18.45 · Original synthesis by Rahul S.\
</p>';
}

// ── Five Dharmas ──
function dhBuildFiveDharmas(){
  return '\
<p style="font-family:\'Cormorant Garamond\',serif;font-size:17px;line-height:1.85;color:rgba(245,230,210,.94);font-style:italic;margin-bottom:30px;">\
Dharm is one. But it shows up at five different levels of generality, depending on what you are asking and from where. The tradition names them carefully so you do not confuse the universal with the particular, or the personal with the emergency.\
</p>\
\
<h3 style="font-family:\'Cinzel\',serif;font-size:15px;letter-spacing:3px;color:rgba(255,210,130,.98);margin:30px 0 8px;">1 · SANĀTANA DHARMA</h3>\
<div style="font-family:\'Noto Sans Devanagari\',serif;font-size:14px;color:rgba(255,210,160,.7);letter-spacing:2px;margin-bottom:14px;">सनातन धर्म · the eternal foundation</div>\
<p style="font-family:\'Cormorant Garamond\',serif;font-size:16.5px;line-height:1.85;color:rgba(225,215,190,.9);">\
The dharm <em>by which the universe is the universe</em>. Fire burns; water wets; gravity pulls; cause produces effect; truth survives, lies unravel; a seed becomes a tree; a tree falls and feeds the soil for the next seed. <strong>This dharm has no birthday and no death-day.</strong> It is not Hindu, not Buddhist, not Christian, not anything that needed to be invented. It was operating before any name for it existed. It will be operating after every name has been forgotten. <em>Sanātana means "without beginning, without end".</em> This is the level the seers were pointing at when they said the tradition itself is sanātana — meaning <strong>not "Hindu" but the eternal nature of being</strong>.\
</p>\
\
<h3 style="font-family:\'Cinzel\',serif;font-size:15px;letter-spacing:3px;color:rgba(255,210,130,.98);margin:30px 0 8px;">2 · SĀMĀNYA DHARMA</h3>\
<div style="font-family:\'Noto Sans Devanagari\',serif;font-size:14px;color:rgba(255,210,160,.7);letter-spacing:2px;margin-bottom:14px;">सामान्य धर्म · the general / common dharm</div>\
<p style="font-family:\'Cormorant Garamond\',serif;font-size:16.5px;line-height:1.85;color:rgba(225,215,190,.9);">\
The dharm <em>that applies to every human being, regardless of role, age, occupation, or station</em>. The Mahābhārata\'s great list — <em>ahiṃsā</em> (do not harm), <em>satya</em> (do not lie), <em>asteya</em> (do not steal), <em>śauca</em> (be clean inside and out), <em>dama</em> (govern your appetites), <em>kṣamā</em> (forgive), <em>dāna</em> (give), <em>ārjava</em> (be straight, not crooked) — these are the floor. They are not negotiable by occupation. <strong>A king who lies as policy has broken sāmānya dharma. A teacher who steals from students has broken sāmānya dharma. A monk who is cruel has broken sāmānya dharma.</strong> <em>No role exempts a person from being decent.</em>\
</p>\
\
<h3 style="font-family:\'Cinzel\',serif;font-size:15px;letter-spacing:3px;color:rgba(255,210,130,.98);margin:30px 0 8px;">3 · VIŚEṢA DHARMA</h3>\
<div style="font-family:\'Noto Sans Devanagari\',serif;font-size:14px;color:rgba(255,210,160,.7);letter-spacing:2px;margin-bottom:14px;">विशेष धर्म · the particular / role-specific dharm</div>\
<p style="font-family:\'Cormorant Garamond\',serif;font-size:16.5px;line-height:1.85;color:rgba(225,215,190,.9);">\
The dharm of <em>a specific role, station, or stage of life</em>. The dharm of a soldier in war is not the dharm of a doctor in surgery. <strong>A soldier may have to take life. A doctor must not.</strong> A child\'s dharma is to learn, a parent\'s is to teach, an elder\'s is to bless and step back. The four <em>varṇa</em> (work-temperaments) and four <em>āśrama</em> (life-stages) sit here — not as a caste system imposed at birth, but as <em>functional categories</em> of "what this kind of role requires." Where it became hereditary and oppressive, it became distorted; that distortion is itself <em>adharma</em>. <em>The original idea was simple: every role has a particular shape; do that shape well.</em>\
</p>\
\
<h3 style="font-family:\'Cinzel\',serif;font-size:15px;letter-spacing:3px;color:rgba(255,210,130,.98);margin:30px 0 8px;">4 · SVA-DHARMA</h3>\
<div style="font-family:\'Noto Sans Devanagari\',serif;font-size:14px;color:rgba(255,210,160,.7);letter-spacing:2px;margin-bottom:14px;">स्वधर्म · the dharm that is your own</div>\
<p style="font-family:\'Cormorant Garamond\',serif;font-size:16.5px;line-height:1.85;color:rgba(225,215,190,.9);">\
The dharm <em>of you — exactly you, this person, this body, this mind, this set of capacities, this particular life</em>. Not the dharm of the role abstractly, but the dharm of the particular human in this particular moment with this particular history. <strong>This is what Krishna spends most of the Bhagavad Gītā telling Arjuna about.</strong> <em>śreyān svadharmo viguṇaḥ paradharmāt svanuṣṭhitāt</em> — "better one\'s own dharma, even imperfectly done, than another\'s dharma performed perfectly". (BG 3.35; 18.47.) Why? <strong>Because no one else can do you. If you spend your life trying to be someone else, even to perfection, the world has lost the one thing only you could have given.</strong>\
</p>\
<p style="font-family:\'Cormorant Garamond\',serif;font-size:16.5px;line-height:1.85;color:rgba(225,215,190,.9);">\
Sva-dharma is the hardest of the five to find — because it requires <em>knowing yourself</em>, which most lives are arranged to prevent. The work of much of the inner tradition (yoga, jñāna, dhyāna) is to clear the noise enough to hear what your own dharm actually is. <strong>It rarely matches what your parents wanted, what the market rewards, or what is easy.</strong>\
</p>\
\
<h3 style="font-family:\'Cinzel\',serif;font-size:15px;letter-spacing:3px;color:rgba(255,210,130,.98);margin:30px 0 8px;">5 · ĀPAD DHARMA</h3>\
<div style="font-family:\'Noto Sans Devanagari\',serif;font-size:14px;color:rgba(255,210,160,.7);letter-spacing:2px;margin-bottom:14px;">आपद् धर्म · the dharm of emergency</div>\
<p style="font-family:\'Cormorant Garamond\',serif;font-size:16.5px;line-height:1.85;color:rgba(225,215,190,.9);">\
The dharm <em>that becomes valid in extremity</em> — when the normal rules cannot apply because the conditions have collapsed. A starving man may eat what he would not normally eat. A father whose child is dying may borrow without permission to save the life. A society at war may take measures that peace would forbid. <strong>Āpad-dharma is not a free pass to do anything.</strong> It is the <em>narrowly bounded permission to deviate from ordinary dharma to preserve a higher dharma</em> — life itself, the body, the family, the nation, the future. The conditions are strict: the emergency must be real, the deviation must be the smallest possible, and once the emergency passes, ordinary dharma resumes. <em>The Manusmṛti devotes whole chapters to āpad-dharma because the seers knew it would be abused as cover for ordinary selfishness.</em>\
</p>\
\
<h3 style="font-family:\'Cinzel\',serif;font-size:14px;letter-spacing:3px;color:rgba(255,210,130,.95);margin:34px 0 12px;">HOW THE FIVE NEST</h3>\
<p style="font-family:\'Cormorant Garamond\',serif;font-size:16.5px;line-height:1.85;color:rgba(225,215,190,.9);">\
The five nest, like rings around a centre. <strong>Sanātana</strong> is the universal ground — the laws by which anything exists at all. <strong>Sāmānya</strong> is the human floor — the basics every person owes every other. <strong>Viśeṣa</strong> is the role — what your station requires. <strong>Sva-dharma</strong> is the particular flame — what only you can do. And <strong>āpad-dharma</strong> is the emergency override — narrow, bounded, and only when the foundations themselves are at stake.\
</p>\
<p style="font-family:\'Cormorant Garamond\',serif;font-size:16.5px;line-height:1.85;color:rgba(225,215,190,.9);">\
A wise life moves between them constantly. <em>You eat — using sāmānya dharma to not steal. You teach — using viśeṣa dharma of the teacher. You teach in your own particular way — using sva-dharma. You skip teaching one day to take a sick parent to hospital — invoking āpad-dharma. And underneath all of it, fire still burns and water still wets and the cell still heals — sanātana dharma, untouched.</em>\
</p>\
<p style="font-family:\'IBM Plex Mono\',monospace;font-size:8px;letter-spacing:2px;color:rgba(180,140,80,.6);text-transform:uppercase;margin-top:30px;padding-top:18px;border-top:1px solid rgba(220,160,80,.1);">\
References · Manusmṛti X.81-118 · Mahābhārata Śānti Parva 109, 261 · Bhagavad Gītā 3.35, 18.47 · Yājñavalkya Smṛti III · Original synthesis by Rahul S.\
</p>';
}

// ── Dharm vs Religion ──
function dhBuildPanth(){
  return '\
<p style="font-family:\'Cormorant Garamond\',serif;font-size:17px;line-height:1.85;color:rgba(245,230,210,.94);font-style:italic;margin-bottom:30px;">\
The single mistranslation that has caused the most confusion in modern times: rendering <em>dharm</em> as "religion". They are not the same thing. Not even close. The Sanskrit and Hindi tradition has another word for what English calls religion — <strong>panth</strong> — and the difference between dharm and panth is the difference between <em>physics</em> and <em>a particular textbook teaching physics</em>.\
</p>\
\
<h3 style="font-family:\'Cinzel\',serif;font-size:15px;letter-spacing:3px;color:rgba(255,210,130,.98);margin:28px 0 8px;">DHARM</h3>\
<p style="font-family:\'Cormorant Garamond\',serif;font-size:16.5px;line-height:1.85;color:rgba(225,215,190,.9);">\
The <strong>foundational nature</strong> of a thing or a being. Eternal. Not invented. Not chosen. Not voted on. Not handed down by a council. <em>It is what makes a thing be the thing it is — and what makes the universe coherent enough to have things in it at all.</em> Fire burns, water wets, gravity pulls, the cell heals, the tree grows, the mother nourishes, the warrior protects, the teacher gives. None of this requires a text. None of this requires a temple. None of this requires belief. <strong>Dharm operates whether or not anyone notices it.</strong>\
</p>\
\
<h3 style="font-family:\'Cinzel\',serif;font-size:15px;letter-spacing:3px;color:rgba(255,210,130,.98);margin:28px 0 8px;">PANTH</h3>\
<p style="font-family:\'Cormorant Garamond\',serif;font-size:16.5px;line-height:1.85;color:rgba(225,215,190,.9);">\
A <strong>path</strong>, a <strong>school</strong>, a <strong>set of practices and rules and customs</strong> chosen by a community. Always invented. Always specific. Always with a founder, a date, a region, a language. Christianity is a panth. Islam is a panth. Sikhism is a panth. The various sectarian movements within so-called Hinduism — Vaiṣṇava panth, Śaiva panth, Śākta panth, Smārta panth — are panths. <em>Each panth is a particular way of pointing at the underlying dharm.</em> Each has rituals, a calendar, a liturgy, a clergy, a costume, a holiday cycle, a do-and-don\'t list. <strong>These are valuable. They are useful. They organise communities. They train minds. They preserve memory.</strong> <em>But none of them is dharm.</em> Each one is a finger pointing at dharm. The finger is not the moon.\
</p>\
\
<div style="background:rgba(220,160,80,.04);border-left:2px solid rgba(220,160,80,.4);padding:18px 22px;margin:30px 0;">\
<div style="font-family:\'IBM Plex Mono\',monospace;font-size:8px;letter-spacing:3px;color:rgba(220,180,120,.7);text-transform:uppercase;margin-bottom:12px;">A SIDE-BY-SIDE</div>\
<table style="width:100%;border-collapse:collapse;font-family:\'Cormorant Garamond\',serif;color:rgba(225,215,190,.92);font-size:15px;">\
<tr style="border-bottom:1px solid rgba(220,160,80,.15);"><td style="padding:8px 6px;width:50%;"><strong>Dharm</strong></td><td style="padding:8px 6px;"><strong>Panth (Religion)</strong></td></tr>\
<tr><td style="padding:6px;">Eternal — was, is, will be</td><td style="padding:6px;">Historical — has a founder, a date</td></tr>\
<tr><td style="padding:6px;">The nature of things</td><td style="padding:6px;">A teaching about that nature</td></tr>\
<tr><td style="padding:6px;">Operates without belief</td><td style="padding:6px;">Often requires belief</td></tr>\
<tr><td style="padding:6px;">Universal — applies to all beings</td><td style="padding:6px;">Particular — chosen by a community</td></tr>\
<tr><td style="padding:6px;">No clergy needed</td><td style="padding:6px;">Usually has clergy</td></tr>\
<tr><td style="padding:6px;">No conversion possible</td><td style="padding:6px;">Conversion is the central act</td></tr>\
<tr><td style="padding:6px;">Cannot be exclusive</td><td style="padding:6px;">Often claims exclusivity</td></tr>\
<tr><td style="padding:6px;">A river</td><td style="padding:6px;">A particular cup</td></tr>\
<tr><td style="padding:6px;">Knowing</td><td style="padding:6px;">Believing</td></tr>\
<tr><td style="padding:6px;">Discovered</td><td style="padding:6px;">Designed</td></tr>\
</table>\
</div>\
\
<h3 style="font-family:\'Cinzel\',serif;font-size:14px;letter-spacing:3px;color:rgba(255,210,130,.95);margin:32px 0 12px;">WHY THIS MATTERS</h3>\
<p style="font-family:\'Cormorant Garamond\',serif;font-size:16.5px;line-height:1.85;color:rgba(225,215,190,.9);">\
When dharm got translated as "religion", three damages followed. <strong>(1)</strong> The Sanātana tradition — which had always presented itself as a description of the nature of reality, available to anyone — got reduced to "the Hindu religion", as if it were just another club. <strong>(2)</strong> Conflicts between panths got mapped onto dharm itself, so people began to fight about <em>whose dharm was correct</em> — a sentence that, in the original meaning, makes no sense at all. <strong>Fire does not have a denomination.</strong> <strong>(3)</strong> Modern people, suspicious of "religion", threw out dharm with it — losing the most important word their ancestors had for the foundational nature of being.\
</p>\
\
<h3 style="font-family:\'Cinzel\',serif;font-size:14px;letter-spacing:3px;color:rgba(255,210,130,.95);margin:32px 0 12px;">A WORKING SENTENCE</h3>\
<p style="font-family:\'Cormorant Garamond\',serif;font-size:18px;line-height:1.95;color:rgba(255,225,180,.96);text-align:center;font-style:italic;margin:24px 0;padding:0 12px;">\
You can leave a panth. You cannot leave dharm — because dharm is what you are made of.\
</p>\
\
<h3 style="font-family:\'Cinzel\',serif;font-size:14px;letter-spacing:3px;color:rgba(255,210,130,.95);margin:32px 0 12px;">CAN A PANTH BE DHĀRMIC?</h3>\
<p style="font-family:\'Cormorant Garamond\',serif;font-size:16.5px;line-height:1.85;color:rgba(225,215,190,.9);">\
Yes — when its rituals, its rules, and its community life <strong>support</strong> the practitioner in living according to dharm. The morning lamp lit before the household shrine, the silence kept on the day of fasting, the verse memorised in childhood — these can shape a person into someone whose first instincts <em>are</em> dharmic. The panth is the trellis; dharm is the vine. <em>A good panth keeps the vine alive.</em>\
</p>\
<p style="font-family:\'Cormorant Garamond\',serif;font-size:16.5px;line-height:1.85;color:rgba(225,215,190,.9);">\
And a panth becomes <em>adharmic</em> when it cuts itself off from the foundation it was supposed to be supporting — when it begins to demand belief instead of pointing at truth, when it rewards loyalty instead of cultivating insight, when it turns its rituals into instruments of fear, when it claims exclusive access to the eternal. <strong>At that point the trellis has strangled the vine.</strong> Reform, in every panth, is exactly the work of cutting the trellis back and freeing the vine.\
</p>\
\
<p style="font-family:\'Cormorant Garamond\',serif;font-size:16.5px;line-height:1.85;color:rgba(225,215,190,.9);">\
This is why my project carries the name <strong>Living Sanātan Timeline</strong> and not "Hindu Timeline". <em>Sanātan dharm is not the property of any community.</em> It is the foundation under every honest community. To explore it is to explore being itself.\
</p>\
<p style="font-family:\'IBM Plex Mono\',monospace;font-size:8px;letter-spacing:2px;color:rgba(180,140,80,.6);text-transform:uppercase;margin-top:30px;padding-top:18px;border-top:1px solid rgba(220,160,80,.1);">\
References · Yāska\'s Nirukta on the word dharm · Manusmṛti II.9-12 · Original synthesis by Rahul S.\
</p>';
}

// ── The Hundred Things ──
function dhBuildThousand(){
  // Build directly from DHARM_CATEGORIES — the same 100 things you can tap on the canvas.
  var html = '\
<p style="font-family:\'Cormorant Garamond\',serif;font-size:18px;line-height:1.9;color:rgba(245,230,210,.95);font-style:italic;margin-bottom:14px;">\
A hundred things — and what each, by being itself, must do.\
</p>\
<p style="font-family:\'Cormorant Garamond\',serif;font-size:16.5px;line-height:1.85;color:rgba(225,215,190,.92);margin-bottom:30px;">\
This is not a complete list. It cannot be. Reality keeps revealing new things. The exercise is to do this for any thing you encounter — to ask <em>what is this, and what does it do without being told?</em> That is its <strong>dharm</strong>. The number a hundred is small enough to read in a sitting, large enough to feel the pattern: every thing has one, no two are the same, and yet the act of having one is the same act. Every entry below also lives as an orb on the experience canvas — tap one there, read it here.\
</p>';
  for (var ci=0; ci<DHARM_CATEGORIES.length; ci++){
    var cat = DHARM_CATEGORIES[ci];
    var col = cat.col + '0.95)';
    var dim = cat.col + '0.55)';
    var bright = cat.cBright + '1)';
    html += '\
<div style="margin:38px 0 22px 0;padding-bottom:10px;border-bottom:1px solid '+cat.col+'0.18);">\
<div style="display:flex;align-items:baseline;gap:14px;flex-wrap:wrap;">\
<span style="font-family:\'Cinzel\',serif;font-size:11px;letter-spacing:5px;color:'+dim+';text-transform:uppercase;">'+(ci+1).toString().padStart(2,'0')+'</span>\
<span style="font-family:\'Cinzel\',serif;font-size:21px;letter-spacing:2px;color:'+bright+';">'+cat.label+'</span>\
<span style="font-family:\'IBM Plex Mono\',monospace;font-size:10px;letter-spacing:3px;color:'+dim+';text-transform:uppercase;">· '+cat.sub+' · '+cat.things.length+' things</span>\
</div>\
</div>\
<div style="display:grid;grid-template-columns:1fr;gap:0;">';
    for (var ti=0; ti<cat.things.length; ti++){
      var t = cat.things[ti];
      html += '\
<div style="display:grid;grid-template-columns:130px 1fr;gap:18px;padding:11px 0;border-bottom:1px dashed rgba(180,140,80,.08);align-items:baseline;">\
<div style="display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:2px;">\
<span style="font-family:\'Cinzel\',serif;font-size:14.5px;letter-spacing:.5px;color:'+col+';">'+t.n+'</span>\
<span style="font-family:\'Noto Sans Devanagari\',\'Sanskrit Text\',serif;font-size:13px;color:'+dim+';">'+t.skt+'</span>\
</div>\
<div style="font-family:\'Cormorant Garamond\',serif;font-size:16px;line-height:1.7;color:rgba(235,225,205,.92);font-style:italic;">'+t.d+'</div>\
</div>';
    }
    html += '</div>';
  }
  html += '\
<p style="font-family:\'Cormorant Garamond\',serif;font-size:16.5px;line-height:1.85;color:rgba(225,215,190,.9);margin-top:36px;">\
A hundred is the doorway, not the room. <strong>What you cannot find on this list, look at directly. Ask: what is this thing, and what does it do without instruction? — that is its dharm.</strong> The exercise is the practice. After a while, you stop needing the list.\
</p>\
<p style="font-family:\'IBM Plex Mono\',monospace;font-size:8px;letter-spacing:2px;color:rgba(180,140,80,.6);text-transform:uppercase;margin-top:30px;padding-top:18px;border-top:1px solid rgba(220,160,80,.1);">\
Compiled by Rahul S. · May 2026 · Drawn from Vedānta, Sāṅkhya, modern biology, classical physics, lived observation · The list is open-ended.\
</p>';
  return html;
}


// ── Cell That Heals ──
function dhBuildCell(){
  return '\
<p style="font-family:\'Cormorant Garamond\',serif;font-size:17.5px;line-height:1.9;color:rgba(245,230,210,.95);font-style:italic;margin-bottom:30px;">\
The single example most worth dwelling on. The single example that — if you take it seriously — replaces every theological argument about whether dharm exists.\
</p>\
\
<h3 style="font-family:\'Cinzel\',serif;font-size:14px;letter-spacing:3px;color:rgba(255,210,130,.95);margin:24px 0 12px;">THE WOUND</h3>\
<p style="font-family:\'Cormorant Garamond\',serif;font-size:16.5px;line-height:1.85;color:rgba(225,215,190,.9);">\
You cut your hand. The skin opens. Blood comes. <em>Within seconds — long before you have decided what to do — your body has already begun the response.</em>\
</p>\
\
<h3 style="font-family:\'Cinzel\',serif;font-size:14px;letter-spacing:3px;color:rgba(255,210,130,.95);margin:24px 0 12px;">SECOND ZERO TO SECOND TEN</h3>\
<p style="font-family:\'Cormorant Garamond\',serif;font-size:16.5px;line-height:1.85;color:rgba(225,215,190,.9);">\
Damaged cells release distress signals — adenosine, ATP, calcium ions, fragments of cellular machinery — into the surrounding tissue. <strong>Platelets in the bloodstream — small cell fragments with no nucleus, no brain of any sort — recognise the exposed collagen of broken vessel walls and stick to it.</strong> Within a minute they have aggregated, released their own signalling chemicals, and triggered the cascade of clotting factors. Fibrin threads weave a mesh. Bleeding stops. <em>None of these particles knows that there is a "you" they are saving.</em>\
</p>\
\
<h3 style="font-family:\'Cinzel\',serif;font-size:14px;letter-spacing:3px;color:rgba(255,210,130,.95);margin:24px 0 12px;">MINUTE ONE TO MINUTE THIRTY</h3>\
<p style="font-family:\'Cormorant Garamond\',serif;font-size:16.5px;line-height:1.85;color:rgba(225,215,190,.9);">\
Vessels nearby constrict, then dilate, bringing more blood to the area. Neutrophils — white blood cells — squeeze out of the capillaries and arrive at the wound site, devouring bacteria and debris. <strong>Each neutrophil is a single cell. It has no education. It has no orders from a central command. It is following local chemical gradients.</strong> And it is doing exactly the right thing at exactly the right time.\
</p>\
\
<h3 style="font-family:\'Cinzel\',serif;font-size:14px;letter-spacing:3px;color:rgba(255,210,130,.95);margin:24px 0 12px;">DAY ONE TO DAY THREE</h3>\
<p style="font-family:\'Cormorant Garamond\',serif;font-size:16.5px;line-height:1.85;color:rgba(225,215,190,.9);">\
Macrophages arrive. They eat the dead neutrophils, the debris, the broken cells. They also release growth factors that begin the next phase. Fibroblasts — cells whose entire job is to manufacture collagen — migrate into the wound from the surrounding tissue and begin to synthesise the protein scaffolding that will become new skin. <em>The fibroblast does not deliberate.</em> It does not weigh costs and benefits. It enters the wound and begins making collagen because that is what fibroblasts in a wound bed do. <strong>It is following its dharm.</strong>\
</p>\
\
<h3 style="font-family:\'Cinzel\',serif;font-size:14px;letter-spacing:3px;color:rgba(255,210,130,.95);margin:24px 0 12px;">DAY THREE TO DAY TWENTY-ONE</h3>\
<p style="font-family:\'Cormorant Garamond\',serif;font-size:16.5px;line-height:1.85;color:rgba(225,215,190,.9);">\
New blood vessels begin to form by angiogenesis — endothelial cells sprouting from existing capillaries, drawn by chemical signals into the wound bed. Epithelial cells at the wound\'s edge proliferate and migrate inward, sealing the surface. The wound contracts. Granulation tissue gives way to scar. By the third week, the visible repair is largely complete; <em>the invisible remodelling will continue for a year.</em>\
</p>\
\
<div style="background:rgba(220,160,80,.05);border-left:2px solid rgba(220,160,80,.4);padding:24px 26px;margin:34px 0;">\
<h3 style="font-family:\'Cinzel\',serif;font-size:14px;letter-spacing:3px;color:rgba(255,210,130,.98);margin:0 0 14px;">WHAT THIS MEANS</h3>\
<p style="font-family:\'Cormorant Garamond\',serif;font-size:17px;line-height:1.95;color:rgba(245,230,210,.95);font-style:italic;margin-bottom:14px;">\
At no point in the entire process did any cell receive an instruction it could understand as an instruction. The platelet did not consult a textbook. The neutrophil did not pray. The fibroblast did not ask permission. <strong>Each cell did what it does.</strong> And by each cell doing what it does — without coordination, without theology, without philosophy — <em>your hand healed.</em>\
</p>\
<p style="font-family:\'Cormorant Garamond\',serif;font-size:16.5px;line-height:1.85;color:rgba(225,215,190,.92);">\
This is the single clearest demonstration of dharm available to anyone who has ever scraped a knee. <strong>Dharm does not require belief.</strong> It does not require permission. It does not require a priest. It is the very nature of cells — to be cells, in the body, in the world — that makes the body work at all.\
</p>\
</div>\
\
<h3 style="font-family:\'Cinzel\',serif;font-size:14px;letter-spacing:3px;color:rgba(255,210,130,.95);margin:24px 0 12px;">SCALING UP</h3>\
<p style="font-family:\'Cormorant Garamond\',serif;font-size:16.5px;line-height:1.85;color:rgba(225,215,190,.9);">\
Now extend the example. <strong>What is true of one cell in the wound is true of every cell, every organ, every body, every relationship, every village, every nation, every ecosystem.</strong> When each part does what its nature requires — without abandoning its place to imitate another part, without refusing to do what it is for, without pretending to be something else — <em>the whole functions, the whole heals, the whole grows.</em>\
</p>\
<p style="font-family:\'Cormorant Garamond\',serif;font-size:16.5px;line-height:1.85;color:rgba(225,215,190,.9);">\
And when a part refuses its dharm — a cell that divides without stopping is called a <strong>cancer</strong>; a person who refuses their nature becomes ill in some other way; a profession that abandons its standard becomes corrupt; a society that forgets what it is for falls. <em>The structure of the failure is identical at every scale.</em>\
</p>\
\
<h3 style="font-family:\'Cinzel\',serif;font-size:14px;letter-spacing:3px;color:rgba(255,210,130,.95);margin:24px 0 12px;">THE PRACTICE</h3>\
<p style="font-family:\'Cormorant Garamond\',serif;font-size:16.5px;line-height:1.85;color:rgba(225,215,190,.9);">\
Next time you cut yourself, watch. Or remember a time you have. <strong>Notice that the entire repair happened without you.</strong> You did not have to want it. You did not have to deserve it. You did not have to belong to a religion. The cells did their dharm. <em>Every breath you have ever taken was, similarly, taken by your body without instruction. Every meal was digested without you supervising the enzymes. Your heart has never asked permission to beat.</em>\
</p>\
<p style="font-family:\'Cormorant Garamond\',serif;font-size:18px;line-height:1.95;color:rgba(255,225,180,.96);text-align:center;font-style:italic;margin:30px 0;padding:0 12px;">\
Dharm is doing its work in you, right now. The only question is whether the conscious you joins it, or fights it.\
</p>\
<p style="font-family:\'IBM Plex Mono\',monospace;font-size:8px;letter-spacing:2px;color:rgba(180,140,80,.6);text-transform:uppercase;margin-top:30px;padding-top:18px;border-top:1px solid rgba(220,160,80,.1);">\
References · Gurtner et al., Wound repair and regeneration · Charaka Saṃhitā · Suśruta Saṃhitā · Yoga Sūtras II.18 · Original synthesis by Rahul S.\
</p>';
}

// ── Notes ──
function dhBuildNotes(){
  return '\
<h3 style="font-family:\'Cinzel\',serif;font-size:14px;letter-spacing:3px;color:rgba(255,210,130,.95);margin:18px 0 12px;">A NOTE FROM THE BUILDER</h3>\
<p style="font-family:\'Cormorant Garamond\',serif;font-size:16.5px;line-height:1.85;color:rgba(225,215,190,.92);">\
This page is my attempt to put what I actually believe about dharm onto a screen. <em>It is not authoritative.</em> It is a personal synthesis, drawn from the texts I have read, the teachers I have listened to, the body I have lived in, the years I have failed to live in alignment with what I most knew. I have tried to keep the language as close to the ground as I can. <strong>Where the words sound abstract, the things are concrete.</strong> Sit with a flame. Watch a cut heal. Read the list slowly. The understanding does not come from being told. It comes from looking.\
</p>\
\
<h3 style="font-family:\'Cinzel\',serif;font-size:14px;letter-spacing:3px;color:rgba(255,210,130,.95);margin:28px 0 12px;">CORE TEXTUAL SOURCES</h3>\
<ul style="font-family:\'Cormorant Garamond\',serif;font-size:15.5px;line-height:1.95;color:rgba(225,215,190,.9);padding-left:20px;">\
<li><strong>Manusmṛti</strong> · especially II.6, II.9-12, X.81-118 on the levels of dharma and on emergency dharma</li>\
<li><strong>Mahābhārata</strong> · Śānti Parva 109, 261; Anuśāsana Parva on the universals of conduct</li>\
<li><strong>Bhagavad Gītā</strong> · 2.31-37 on viśeṣa-dharma; 3.35 and 18.45-47 on sva-dharma; 18.66 on letting go of all dharmas</li>\
<li><strong>Yājñavalkya Smṛti</strong> · I-III for the formal classification</li>\
<li><strong>Yāska\'s Nirukta</strong> · for the etymological root of <em>dhṛ</em> — to hold, to support, to bear</li>\
<li><strong>Yoga Sūtras</strong> · for the inner consequences of acting in alignment with one\'s nature</li>\
<li><strong>Charaka Saṃhitā · Suśruta Saṃhitā</strong> · for the physiology of healing as dharm in action</li>\
</ul>\
\
<h3 style="font-family:\'Cinzel\',serif;font-size:14px;letter-spacing:3px;color:rgba(255,210,130,.95);margin:28px 0 12px;">THE WORD ITSELF</h3>\
<p style="font-family:\'Cormorant Garamond\',serif;font-size:16.5px;line-height:1.85;color:rgba(225,215,190,.9);">\
<em>Dharma</em> (<span style="font-family:\'Noto Sans Devanagari\';">धर्म</span>) is from the Sanskrit root <strong>dhṛ</strong> (<span style="font-family:\'Noto Sans Devanagari\';">धृ</span>), meaning <em>to hold, to support, to bear, to maintain</em>. The same root that gives <em>dhāraṇā</em> (concentration, holding in place) and <em>dharaṇī</em> (the earth, that which bears all). The earliest layered usage in the <em>Ṛgveda</em> already carries a double sense: <strong>that which upholds the cosmic order</strong>, and <strong>that which a particular thing must do to be itself</strong>. The two senses were never separate in the original. The English "religion" is from <em>religare</em>, to bind back. <em>Dharma</em> is not about binding. It is about supporting from below.\
</p>\
\
<h3 style="font-family:\'Cinzel\',serif;font-size:14px;letter-spacing:3px;color:rgba(255,210,130,.95);margin:28px 0 12px;">RELATED PAGES ON THIS SITE</h3>\
<p style="font-family:\'Cormorant Garamond\',serif;font-size:15.5px;line-height:1.85;color:rgba(225,215,190,.9);">\
The <strong>Dharmic Mirror</strong> takes you through your own dharma quiz; the <strong>Dharma Compass</strong> helps locate where you are in your own svadharma; the <strong>Karma Loop</strong> shows the consequence-side of dharm; the <strong>Pañca Mahābhūta</strong> page explores the dharm of the five elements in their own canvas. This page is meant to sit underneath all of them as the foundation each was assuming.\
</p>\
\
<h3 style="font-family:\'Cinzel\',serif;font-size:14px;letter-spacing:3px;color:rgba(255,210,130,.95);margin:28px 0 12px;">A FINAL LINE</h3>\
<p style="font-family:\'Cormorant Garamond\',serif;font-size:18px;line-height:1.95;color:rgba(255,225,180,.96);text-align:center;font-style:italic;margin:30px 0;padding:0 12px;">\
You have been doing dharm with every breath of your life. The work now is to do it on purpose.\
</p>\
<p style="font-family:\'IBM Plex Mono\',monospace;font-size:8px;letter-spacing:2px;color:rgba(180,140,80,.6);text-transform:uppercase;margin-top:30px;padding-top:18px;border-top:1px solid rgba(220,160,80,.1);">\
Dharm — The Foundation of All Things · Built May 2026 · Original synthesis by Rahul S. · livingsanatan.com\
</p>';
}

